## Corrections & Rule Review (replaces the "Compact Session Protocol", 2026-10-10)

The old rule forced at least 2 new hooks or skills on every `/compact`. It produced accumulation, not
learning, and it contradicts "minimal context". Two rules replace it. Both are prompt-level (no hook
enforces them).

1. **Write a correction down at once.** When the user corrects you, a verifier rejects your work, or you
   catch yourself repeating a mistake: append an entry to `tasks/lessons.md` → "Pending" in that same
   turn (format in that file). At close, promote each entry to where it will actually be read: a
   one-line rule in the project's `CLAUDE.md`, a skill when the fix is a procedure, GOTCHAS for a
   reproducible bug, MEMORY for a pattern. Prefer editing or deleting an existing rule, hook or skill over
   adding one; add a hook only when a prompt-level rule has already failed. Zero new items is a valid
   `/compact`.
2. **Be willing to throw rules away.** A rule, hook or skill that exists to compensate for a model
   weakness is scaffolding, and a stronger model may not need it. When the model generation changes (or
   when asked), run the Rule Review (Agent Guide §26): list the scaffolding, remove one piece at a time,
   measure before and after, delete what no longer pays for itself, log it in `tasks/lessons.md` →
   "Retired". A rule that cannot be retired is the bug.

