## Enumerate Before You Claim (2026-09-07, learned the hard way)

**A filtered search proves what MATCHED. It can never prove "there is nothing else."** Before "there are no
others", "nothing else references it", "it cannot come back" or "it is safe to delete": list the whole
surface first and then narrow; resolve each target (does the file it points at still exist?); search for
what RE-CREATES it; then say what you measured, not what you infer. Tool (read-only):
`~/.claude/hooks/governance/enumerate-before-claiming.sh` (`--broken`, `--creators <dir>`). Applies to
hooks, cron entries, registry keys, env vars, mounts and skills as much as to scheduled tasks. The incident
and the full procedure: Agent Guide §21.

---

## Measure a Fix on the Path It Protects, Under Load (2026-09-15, learned by making it worse)

**A fix that trades a rare failure for a frequent one is a regression, however correct its logic.** Time the
fix on the path it protects, before and after, **under load** (several sessions at once), not on an idle
machine. Ask what it costs when it is WRONG (false-block rate × how often the path runs): a guard that
fires on ordinary work gets switched off. When the measurement contradicts the diagnosis, the measurement
wins. Prefer raising a budget over adding a watchdog. The incident and the five rules: Agent Guide §23.

